    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d600">

                <pb facs="1117" n="1101" sameAs="frus:frus1950v04/pg1101"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.5611/2–1150: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Kirk</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1950-02-11T13:00:00+03:00">February 11,
                            1950—1 p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>474. Soviet press so far silent re H-bomb and specifically related US discussions
                        (Depcirtel February 3, 5 p. m.<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d600-f1"/>). Occasionally carries brief item supporting Soviet
                    position re “prohibiting” A-bomb, growth “peace movement” or “war hysteria US”
                    which evidently based US agitation H-bomb policy,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d600-f2"
                    /> but avoids all reference to prospects US possession such bomb. Thus <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Izvestiya</hi> February 11 in regular section <pb
                        facs="1118" n="1102" xml:id="pg1102"/> devoted “peace movement” mentions
                    petition to Truman signed Thomas Mann,<anchor n="3" xml:id="d600-f3"/> Frederick
                        Schuman<anchor n="4" xml:id="d600-f4"/>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">et al</hi> favoring direct negotiations with USSR save
                    world from “atomic catastrophe”.</p>
                <p>Embassy’s Soviet contacts too few and unrepresentative permit estimate extent
                    awareness H-bomb has leaked to Soviet people via VOA or other sources. Assume this gradually happening, but so far
                    not direct evidence. Believe subject unlikely arouse much popular excitement
                    here (Embtel 2516, October 5, 1949<anchor
                        n="5" xml:id="d600-f5"/>).</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kirk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d600-f1" n="1" xml:id="d600-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d600-f2" n="2" xml:id="d600-f2-content">
                <p>For documentation on the foreign policy aspects of the development of atomic
                    energy by the United States, see <ref target="frus1950v01#pg_493">vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, pp. 493</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d600-f3" n="3" xml:id="d600-f3-content">
                <p>Thomas Mann, German author and novelist resident in the United States at this
                    time.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d600-f4" n="4" xml:id="d600-f4-content">
                <p>Frederick L. Schuman was a professor at Williams College.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d600-f5" n="5" xml:id="d600-f5-content">
                <p>
                    <ref target="frus1949v05#pg_664">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1949, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">v</hi>, p. 664</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
